    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97069.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "So I was in the Capitol at 1 p.m. as the votes debate started on the certification of the election, started in the chamber, then came down stairs on the first floor of the Capitol to have a meeting with former Congressman Ron Wright, who subsequently passed away from COVID. He was we were talking about his interest in being on the Energy and Commerce Committee, that meeting lasted about a half an hour or 40 minutes. And then I went in the tunnel between the Capitol and the office building, the Rayburn House office building, where my office was and went over to the office building. I was the only one of my staff there that day, not because I thought there was going to be trouble, but I knew there was going to be a big crowd. And I told my staff, hey, I can handle things. You guys just take the day off because it's going to be too much hassle to get in and out with all the barricades and with all the people that will likely be on Capitol Hill. So I was the only one there. I went into my office, flipped on Fox News, figured I'd wait for the first vote on certification. We were going to vote state by state. It was going to be a long, long vote. And if there were objections, so there was likely that morning to be seven or eight objections potentially. And so it could have been a very long vote that started somewhere around two or three o'clock and went maybe till 10 or 11 o'clock at night. uh... so i was back in my office a little before to the way i remember it flipped on fox news and they showed the uh... the folks outside the capital and then shortly after that there was a fox news alert that the capital had been breached so I was not in the capital. I was across the street in the Rayburn House office building and just watching it on TV like everybody else. I didn't really feel scared. I was very disappointed at what was happening that people thought turning to violence was a good idea, but I was never in a place or a mindset where I was scared at all, but I did watch the whole thing like most Americans on television as opposed to, you know, live in the Capitol because I was across the street in the Rayburn House office building.",
