    "turn_text": "all. I was not focused on January 6th. I wasn't really even looking for a job, but this job at the chamber, which happens to be in the town I live in, a chance to be involved in policy here in the state of Ohio and around business issues and competitiveness issues, entrepreneurship issues that I care deeply about, the business climate and economic opportunity for all. It was an incredible opportunity to be at home and still make a difference in policy. So I decided to make the move, but it was unrelated to unhappiness in any way with Congress, January 6th, any of that stuff. It was really about the right time for me to be back home with my family. I have an eight-year-old son, a 12-year-old daughter, and I was missing their childhood, and I felt like as I reflected on this opportunity that hadn't been open in 28 years, the previous CEO of the Chamber had been here 28 years, uh... it was a great opportunity to come home make a difference and be with my family every day which was appealing to me so it wasn't about anything related to congress it was just a good time for me to make a change even",
