    "turn_idx": 64,
    "turn_text": "few things we need to do to get people to go back to work. The biggest thing that nobody's really talking about is around childcare and dealing with, you know, dependent children so that two Parents can go back to work if they choose. The capacity in our childcare system is lower than it was at the beginning of the COVID pandemic. And we've got to try to deal with that. We've got to try to build more capacity and figure out how to make it affordable for folks so that somebody making $40,000 or $50,000 a year isn't handing most of their salary back to the daycare provider just to go to work. So there are issues like that. There are issues around skills to make sure people have the right skills for the jobs of today and tomorrow. I mean, if you're skilled in coding or you're skilled in any kind of computer engineering, there are a ton of good paying jobs out there. But there's a lot of people who don't have those kind of skills, even skills in the technical fields like truck driver or in the skilled trades like bricklayer and those kind of things are jobs that are abundant and pay really good wages right now. So we need to figure out how to get people to go into some of those training programs and some of it's cultural because a lot of high school counselors and a lot of parents tell every kid they have to go to college. We're going to have more $100,000 a year jobs in Ohio that don't require a college degree by 2024, more than we've had in 50 years that pay a good wage that don't require a college degree. Now they might require a certification, they might require some type of, you know, a certificate from a community college or a workforce education area but there's a lot of great jobs coming that do not require a college degree. That's not to say we don't want kids to go to college but not everybody should go to college or think they have to go to college just to get a good paying job and there's a ton of economic opportunity and jobs coming that are not just for college graduates. So it's an exciting time to be advocating on behalf of Ohio businesses because there's a lot of economic development and opportunity coming to Ohio in the next couple of years.",
