  {
    "episode_id": 97069,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97069.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 78,
    "turn_text": "a couple things on that I think are really interesting to note. One of the big drivers that has kept legislators, Congressman, from interacting with each other are some of the ethics laws that we've enacted where you can't do a reception or a dinner. And so they don't interact with each other as often as they used to. The other thing that maybe has become politically uh... less popular but is a great way to get to know people is congressional delegation trips so-called co-dels uh... you know i didn't do a lot of those when i was in congress i did five or six i went to the sixty th anniversary of world war two the hundredth anniversary of war war one both in europe I went to Israel. I went to a couple other places during my time in Congress. But when I took those trips, you take them with multiple members of Congress. They have to be of both parties. It's a great way to get to know somebody. You're on a plane with somebody for eight or 15 hours. That's a real opportunity to get to know them. And then you're with them for a few days on the trip. If I was going back to Congress, I'd probably do more congressional delegation trips because you really do get to know people, but I will admit they get politically unpopular because people call them boondoggles or other things",
